- What are alternative approaches if memory constraints are tight for very large coordinate domains?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in fully dynamic Li Chao trees, offline lifetime decomposition, and practical handling of duplicates in production settings.

## Key Concepts
- **Segment tree over time** for lifetime interval decomposition
- **Per-node Li Chao trees** for minimum query aggregation
- **Path-query traversal** across O(log N) nodes
- Memory-time trade-offs for handling large N and duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class LiChao {
  insert(line) { /* insert line into this node's Li Chao tree */ }
  query(x) { /* return minimum y at x across stored lines */ }
}

class DynamicLineSet {
  constructor(N) {
    this.segmentTree = new SegmentTree(N);
  }

  addLine(m, b, id, insertTime) {
    const interval = [insertTime, Infinity];
    this.segmentTree.addInterval(interval, { m, b, id });
  }

  removeLine(id, removeTime) {
    // Update the line's interval end time in the segment tree
    this.segmentTree.closeInterval(id, removeTime);
  }

  queryMin(x, queryTime) {
    return this.segmentTree.queryPath(queryTime)
      .reduce((min, lichao) => Math.min(min, lichao.query(x)), Infinity);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
| Operation | Time | Space |
|-----------|------|-------|
| addLine | O(log N) | O(log N) per line |
| removeLine | O(log N) | - |
| queryMin | O(log² N) | - |

## Edge Cases
- **Identical slopes**: Stored separately with unique ids; Li Chao tree handles min correctly
- **Immediate removal**: Assigned to leaf node only
- **Large N**: Memory scales with O(N log N) total line-node assignments

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to augment a balanced BST to support fast range queries on a multiset with duplicates—a fundamental interview pattern demonstrating understanding of data structure augmentation.

## Key Concepts
- **Treap structure**: Randomized BST with O(log n) expected height
- **Augmented nodes**: cnt tracks duplicates, size maintains subtree element counts
- **Range queries**: countLE(x) finds rank ≤ x; rangeCount derived as countLE(r) - countLE(l-1)
- **Efficient updates**: Size rebalancing occurs only on paths to modified nodes

## Code Example
```python
import random

class TreapNode:
    def __init__(self, key):
        self.key = key
        self.cnt = 1
        self.size = 1
        self.prio = random.random()
        self.left = None
        self.right = None

def update_size(node):
    if node:
        node.size = node.cnt
        if node.left:
            node.size += node.left.size
        if node.right:
            node.size += node.right.size

def rotate_right(p):
    q = p.left
    p.left = q.right
    q.right = p
    update_size(p)
    update_size(q)
    return q

def rotate_left(p):
    q = p.right
    p.right = q.left
    q.left = p
    update_size(p)
    update_size(q)
    return q

def insert(node, key):
    if not node:
        return TreapNode(key)

    if key == node.key:
        node.cnt += 1
    elif key < node.key:
        node.left = insert(node.left, key)
        if node.left.prio < node.prio:
            node = rotate_right(node)
    else:
        node.right = insert(node.right, key)
        if node.right.prio < node.prio:
            node = rotate_left(node)

    update_size(node)
    return node

def count_le(node, x):
    if not node:
        return 0

    if node.key <= x:
        left_size = node.left.size if node.left else 0
        return left_size + node.cnt + count_le(node.right, x)
    else:
        return count_le(node.left, x)

def range_count(node, l, r):
    return count_le(node, r) - count_le(node, l - 1)
```

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates practical data-structure composition: a queue with constant-time min tracking, a common interview pattern for streams and real-time dashboards.

## Key Concepts

- Two-stack queue pattern
- Per-stack min tracking
- Amortized analysis and edge-case handling for duplicates

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinQueue {
  constructor(){ this.inStack = []; this.outStack = []; }
  enqueue(x){ const minIn = this.inStack.length ? Math.min(x, this.inStack[this.inStack.length-1][1]) : x; this.inStack.push([x, minIn]); }
  dequeue(){ if (!this.outStack.length){ while (this.inStack.length){ const [val] = this.inStack.pop(); const minOut = this.outStack.length ? Math.min(val, this.outStack[this.outStack.length-1][1]) : val; this.outStack.push([val, minOut]); } }
    const popped = this.outStack.pop(); return popped ? popped[0] : undefined; }
  getMin(){ const minIn = this.inStack.length ? this.inStack[this.inStack.length-1][1] : Infinity; const minOut = this.outStack.length ? this.outStack[this.outStack.length-1][1] : Infinity; return Math.min(minIn, minOut); }
}
```

